  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Friday)\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nEarly next week, the ridge will have dropped south enough to result \nin northwesterly flow in the upper troposphere. Temperatures will \nstay stubbornly and unseasonably hot during the early week period as \nthe redirected airflow will still be drawing from the warm Mexican \nplateau airmass, but the displaced ridge will enable low-end \nopportunities for an isolated shower or storm over the southern \nEdwards Plateau and Hill Country Sunday afternoon/evening and Monday \nafternoon/evening. Chances remain below 20%.\n\nThe primary feature of interest next week is a trough forecast to \nmove across the southwestern US and Intermountain West on Tuesday, \napproaching our area during the midweek timeframe. There continues \nto be ample disagreement across models regarding the speed of this \ntrough Tuesday onwards and how it interacts with moisture from \nHurricane Polo in the eastern Pacific. A substantial plume of \ntropical moisture is highly likely expected to spread across the \nsouthwestern US, and chances for heavy rain are higher west and \nnorth of our area, but the speed and width of the trough will \ninfluence how much of that deep moisture spreads east into our \nregion. Ensemble cluster analysis suggests that ensemble members \nremain evenly split in the slow/fast trough outcomes, though the EPS \nshows a slight disposition towards the slower solution and the GEFS \nshows a slight disposition towards the faster solution. Some trends \ntowards a slower trough have been noted. Regardless, the overall \npattern is favorable for rain, with chances for widespread rain \nstarting as early as Tuesday and continuing into Friday. It should \nbe noted that these chances are spread across several days due to \nuncertainties in timing, and most of the model QPF fields suggest \nthat the bulk of rain from this potential event will likely \nfall mainly on one or two days during this timeframe rather than\nthrough the entire period.\n\nThe trough is expected to bring along with it a Pacific cold front, \nand as with the trough itself, the timing of the front is uncertain. \nAlthough most models suggest that deep-layer flow won't support a \ncooler airmass cleanly surging into the area, the combination of the \nfront and potential for widespread rains are forecast to drag \ntemperatures closer to near normal, with potential for highs \ndropping into the 80s during the latter half of next week.",
